{The opening was a Gruenfeld Defence...} 21. Qxe7 {, expecting to force his opponent’s resignation at once. But play went on:} ({The worst blow of all was Hollis’s failure to win. In the position shown in the diagram the continuation} 21. Bxb7 Qxb7 22. Qxe8+ Rxe8 23. Rxe8+ {would have left Black helpless against the white rooks (PH Clarke, 1968)}) 21... Rxe7 22. Rxe7 Bxf3 $1 23. Rdd7 Bc6 24. Rxa7 Bxe7 25. Rxe7 Be8 26. b3 b5 27. cxb5 ({This was the second critical point: the question is whether White can win by} 27. Rxe8+ $5 Rxe8 28. cxb5 {. The situation is then by no means as simple as it might appear at first glance, and Hollis, disliking the consequences of} Re4 {, in the end rejected the exchange sacrifice. Analysis afterwards indicated that it would have won, e.g.} 29. Bd6 Rb4 (29... c4 30. b6 $1) 30. a4 Rxb3 31. Bxc5 Kf7 (31... Rb1+ 32. Kh2 Ra1 33. b6 $18) 32. b6 Ke6 33. a5 Kd7 34. a6 Kc8 35. b7+ Kb8 36. Kh2 {etc wins (PHC)}) 27... Bxb5 28. a4 Be8 29. Rc7 Bf7 30. Rxc5 Bxb3 31. a5 Kf7 32. Bd2 Ra7 33. Rc6 Bd5 34. Rd6 Bc4 35. Rc6 Bd5 36. Rd6 Bc4 37. Bc3 Ke7 38. Rb6 Kd7 39. Kh2 Bd5 40. Kg3 Rb7 41. Rxb7+ Bxb7 42. f3 Ke6 43. Kf4 h5 {Source: BCM, January 1968, p2} 1/2-1/2

