{The opening was a Sicilian Defence... The struggle on Board 10 was approximately level until Cortlever also forgot about his most important piece. In the position shown in the diagram he fatally compromised his game by} 29. g4 $2 {, giving Black the opportunity to make a winning combination commencing with} Rxf4 $1 30. Nxf4 ({If} 30. gxh5 {then} Re4+) 30... Qg5 31. Kg2 ({The white knight was lost, for} 31. Kg3 {allows} Nf5+) ({while} 31. Rd4 {is defeated by} Nd5 $1) 31... Qxf4 ({Stockfish 18:} 31... Bxf4 $19) 32. Qxf4 Bxf4 33. c4 b4 34. Kf3 ({Stockfish 18:} 34. c5 $1 dxc5 35. Kf3 Be5 36. Rd7 Kf7 37. Ke4 $11) 34... g5 35. c5 dxc5 36. Rd8+ Kf7 37. Ra8 Ke6 38. Ke4 Bd6 39. Rxa6 Nd5 40. Ra7 Nf6+ 41. Kd3 Kd5 42. a4 bxa3 43. Rxa3 h5 44. gxh5 Nxh5 45. Ke3 Nf4 46. Ra5 Nxh3 {"and Black won easily". Source: BCM, January 1968, p3} 0-1

